          %h3#introduction Introduction
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            is a groupoid internal to some category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space"} path space
            by the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+homotopy"} thin homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            but as the path groupoid is formed by taking a quotient, its structure will vary depending on the choice.
          %p
            This page was started in response to a question on MathOverflow by Theo Johnson-Freyd. The question was “
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/17803/what-is-the-infinite-dimensional-manifold-structure-on-the-space-of-smooth-paths"}> What is the infinite dimensional manifold structure on the space of smooth paths mod thin homotopy?
            ”.
          %h3#linear_structure Linear Structure
          %p
            To find a linear structure, we consider the path groupoid in a vector space, specifically
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            and quotient by the equivalence relation of thin homotopy.
          %p The most important point to realise about the linear structure on this quotient is that it doesn’t exist.
          %p
            The quotient is not by cosets of a subspace and so the linear structure on
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbb{R}^n
            does not descend to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1\mathbb{R}^n
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi cos
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mi cos
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mi s
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi α
                          %mo +
                          %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi cos
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\alpha(t) &amp;= (\cos(2\pi t), 0) \\
                  H_\alpha(s,t) &amp;= (s\cos(2\pi t),0) \\
                  \\beta(t) &amp;= (0,\sin(2\pi t)) \\
                  H_\beta(s,t) &amp;= (0,s\sin(2\pi t)) \\
                  (\alpha + \beta)(t) &amp;= (\cos(2\pi t), \sin(2\pi t))
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            Note that, for simplicity, we are defining the path groupoid as the quotient of
            %strong all
            smooth paths by the corresponding thin homotopical relation. The resulting space is the same as that formed by taking paths with sitting instants, or paths which are flat at the end-points.
          %p
            The reason for this failure is seen when we examine how the sum of two paths is formed. The sum of two arbitrary paths
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            is defined as the composition
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I \to I \times I \to \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n.
          %p
            Here, and hereafter,
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
          %p
            If
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            are thinly homotopic to the zero path, then the obvious way to construct a thin homotopy from the sum to the zero path is to take the sum of the thin homotopies.
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I^2 \to I^2 \times I^2 \to \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n.
          %p
            One cannot compute the exact rank of this without knowing the maps involved, but one can get bounds. In particular, the upper bound on the rank is the minimum of the upper bounds of each piece. The crucial point here is that (unless
            = succeed ")," do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^2 \times I^2 \to \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n
            = succeed "!" do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            Of course, this does not
            %strong prove
            that it does not always work (the counterexample given above does that) since it might be the case that the maps are always such that this upper bound is not achieved. What this does is explain why the existence of the counterexample is not surprising: it would take a considerable number of coincidences for no counterexample to exist.
          %p
            Extending this further, we observe that the process of taking the path groupoid is not a product-preserving functor. This is obvious from considering the case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1\mathbb{R}
            simply has objects
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            with a single morphism between each two objects. However,
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            is much, much larger than the corresponding product as it has plenty of paths not thinly homotopic to each other (even with the same end-points).
          %p
            Finally, the lack of a linear structure on
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1 \mathbb{R}^n
            rules out any interesting manifold structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1 M
          %h3#diffeological_structure Diffeological Structure
          %p
            The diffeological structure is simple to explain. A map
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon U \to P^1M
            is a plot if and only if it locally lifts to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P M
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒱
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            of
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and for each
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒱
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in \mathcal{V}
            a lift of
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi|_V \colon V \to P^1 M
            to a smooth map
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \to P M
          %p
            The fact that plots have to lift locally does mean that it is not always possible to patch together smooth curves, even with suitable reparametrisation. That is, if two smooth curves
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha, \beta \colon I \to P^1 M
            are such that
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(1) = \beta(0)
            and each is flat at that point, then it may not be possible to concatenate them to a smooth path
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo #
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha # \beta
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1 M
            locally lifts to a smooth path in
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P M
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            as maps
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^2 \to M
            with the first parameter being the parameter along the path in
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1 M
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_8de4d29321bda94cd361c4b419afdd232cc629df_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo −
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo −
                          %mi cos
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo ,
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mi cos
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\alpha(s,t) &amp;= \big((1 - s) \sin(2 \pi t),1 - \cos(2 \pi t)\big) \\
                  \\beta(s,t) &amp;= \big(s \sin(2 \pi t), -1 + \cos(2 \pi t)\big)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %svg{:height => "300", :width => "501", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
